  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-119467",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since both fractions have the same denominators, we can combine them into one fraction, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{1-5}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $1-5$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14148354/assets/ac5c43b0ae0187245b0b5f308335e2797483f62afba727e7b64f1bc1dceced77.png)\n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1-5}{6}=\\frac{-4}{6}=-\\frac{4}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{6}=-\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
